WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840
Welcome to Table Talk, where each week I unpack insights from Christian theology and spirituality

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.320
that help you become a thoughtful, contemplative, and just a downright great human being.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.960
My name's Brett Tilford, and today is part two of our series on how to handle relational

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:15.960
conflict.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.360
So, the title of this episode is Bumping Into Myself.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.200
That's a phrase I got from Dr. Frank Green, who taught at the seminary I attended, Portland

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.959
Seminary, and it's been super illuminating for me.

00:00:27.959 --> 00:00:34.240
And the reason why is because it reveals that when I become emotionally reactive, my

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:40.520
tendency is to shift the blame onto someone or something else.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:44.520
For example, I often get mad at my dog, Biscuit.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.520
Why?

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.160
Well, I say it's because he is the worst.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.160
He doesn't obey.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.160
He's so annoying.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.160
He's so terrible.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.320
But the truth is that when I become emotionally reactive to my dog, Biscuit, it's not about

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:58.680
Biscuit.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:00:59.680
Is it?

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:00.680
Who's it about?

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.680
Me.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.720
I want to say it's him, but actually, I'm bumping into myself.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:14.120
And perhaps you have a spouse, a boss, a friend, or an ex-spouse or boss or friend that you

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:20.919
find yourself not merely disagreeing with, but becoming highly emotionally reactive to.

00:01:20.919 --> 00:01:26.980
They just make you so hacked off or depressed or whatever, and what you're tempted to say

00:01:27.639 --> 00:01:28.839
is, of course, it's them.

00:01:28.839 --> 00:01:30.099
It's their bad attitude.

00:01:30.099 --> 00:01:31.300
It's their immaturity.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.500
It's their insensitivity.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.940
It's their utter stupidity.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:38.379
Now, there may be truth in what you're saying.

00:01:38.379 --> 00:01:41.820
In fact, for the sake of argument, I'll go along with it.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:49.580
They can be negative and immature and insensitive and stupid, but that has no bearing on your

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.620
emotional response.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.860
And when you respond in a reactive, angry, insecure, frustrated, fearful way, you're

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:01.779
not bumping into them, you're bumping into you.

00:02:01.779 --> 00:02:05.500
So how does this insight help us navigate conflict?

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.460
Well, bumping into myself helps me realize two things.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:14.660
First, I understand that the issue is rarely the issue.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:19.699
I say the reason I'm freaking out is because of them or this situation, but no.

00:02:19.699 --> 00:02:22.500
The reason I'm freaking out is because of me.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.300
I'm prone to insecurity and fear.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.059
There is an obscure yet illuminating story in the Bible.

00:02:30.059 --> 00:02:31.899
It's found in the book of Numbers, chapter 12.

00:02:31.899 --> 00:02:36.899
You may have heard of Moses, you know, the famous Ten Commandments, let my people go,

00:02:36.899 --> 00:02:38.100
that guy.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.979
So contrary to popular knowledge, he wasn't a lone ranger sort of leader, but he actually

00:02:41.979 --> 00:02:44.979
had two other key leaders who helped him tremendously.

00:02:45.779 --> 00:02:49.580
In fact, they were his brother and his sister, Aaron and Miriam.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:53.300
Now, you probably haven't heard much about Aaron and Miriam because, well, they were

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.860
a bit more behind the scenes, which leads to the story in Numbers, chapter 12, verses

00:02:58.860 --> 00:02:59.860
1 through 2.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:00.860
And I quote,

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:09.979
Miriam and Aaron began to talk against Moses because of his Cushite wife, for he had married

00:03:09.979 --> 00:03:10.979
a Cushite.

00:03:10.979 --> 00:03:18.940
Verse 2, has the Lord spoken only through Moses, they asked, hasn't he also spoken through

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:19.940
us?

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.300
And the Lord heard this.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:27.300
Now, notice the weird juxtaposition of verses 1 and then verse 2, right?

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.740
Verse 1 says they began to talk against Moses.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.419
Now, who were they talking to?

00:03:31.419 --> 00:03:33.380
We're not really sure.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.380
Perhaps simply to one another, though I sort of doubt it.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:37.899
You know how humans are.

00:03:37.899 --> 00:03:41.339
It's likely they began talking to others about Moses.

00:03:41.339 --> 00:03:48.100
And by the way, Cushite means Ethiopian, so it's possible there's some racism or ethnocentrism,

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:52.259
i.e. like cultural superiority going on.

00:03:52.259 --> 00:03:55.220
But then in verse 2, they move on to another topic.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.380
Has the Lord spoken only through Moses?

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.580
Hasn't he spoken through us too?

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.380
Now, we're starting to get closer to the root issue, right?

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.619
Well, was the issue Moses' marriage to a foreigner?

00:04:05.619 --> 00:04:07.419
I don't think so.

00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:11.380
Or was the issue that Moses was the favorite and they were playing second fiddle?

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:15.580
Well, again, I think that's getting closer to the truth, but no.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:20.700
Instead, what was the issue that pushed them to gossip about Moses?

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.260
Their own insecurity.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:27.660
They were bumping into themselves and the issues.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.459
He married a Cushite, and was he the only important one around here?

00:04:31.459 --> 00:04:34.940
They weren't really the primary issues.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.820
It was them and their own feelings of inferiority.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:43.859
That's the first insight that the knowledge that I'm bumping into myself creates.

00:04:43.859 --> 00:04:47.140
All of a sudden, I see the issue is rarely the issue.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:52.100
The second realization that comes as a result of understanding I'm bumping into myself is

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:55.500
that others are bumping into themselves too.

00:04:56.019 --> 00:04:59.820
Now, I want to nuance this because I'm not saying that any time someone comes to you

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:05.059
with feedback, you should say, whoa, Susie, this isn't about me.

00:05:05.059 --> 00:05:06.859
This is about you, my friend.

00:05:06.859 --> 00:05:08.100
Of course not, right?

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.500
Feedback about who we are is essential.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:15.179
But I'm talking about when someone is highly emotionally reactionary, right?

00:05:15.179 --> 00:05:19.019
Once they are freaking out, they've been hijacked.

00:05:19.019 --> 00:05:21.339
So, do not take it personally.

00:05:21.339 --> 00:05:27.899
In fact, the more emotionally reactive they are, it makes it just that much more likely

00:05:27.899 --> 00:05:30.140
that it's not about you at all.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:31.260
It's them.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:35.619
It's their fear, their insecurity, their past pain.

00:05:35.619 --> 00:05:39.739
Now, what's so liberating about this insight is that we tend to do the opposite.

00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:44.380
If someone reacts, then we're like, oh my, oh, I must pay attention to them.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:48.380
Whereas if they're calm, then we think, ah, it's not that big a deal.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:49.779
No, no, no, no.

00:05:49.779 --> 00:05:51.019
It's the opposite.

00:05:51.019 --> 00:05:55.299
If they're calm, take their feedback seriously.

00:05:55.299 --> 00:05:58.980
But if they're freaking out, chill, right?

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:03.179
They're simply bumping into themselves, and it might be wise if you remove yourself from

00:06:03.179 --> 00:06:04.179
that situation.

00:06:04.179 --> 00:06:11.660
So, the next time you're tempted to freak out, remember, you're bumping into yourself.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:15.140
And when someone else is freaking out on you, don't react.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.339
Don't take it personally.

00:06:16.339 --> 00:06:19.339
Just realize they're bumping into themselves too.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:21.140
Well, that's all for today.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:25.179
If you enjoyed this content, please click the Like button and comment below.

00:06:25.179 --> 00:06:28.420
And if you know someone who could benefit from it, go ahead and share it with them.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:29.220
Grace and peace.

